10
00:01:28,853 --> 00:01:35,022
And the discourse is around the five aggregates, which is the body,

11
00:01:35,272 --> 00:01:50,796
feelings, perceptions, concepts, volitional formations, habits of thought, habits of speech, habits of the body, and of course consciousness, which is the screen upon which everything arises.

12
00:01:51,477 --> 00:01:55,164
And the question is, is this a self or is it not a self?

13
00:01:55,183 --> 00:01:58,108
So the Buddha's arguments are always about control,

14
00:01:58,527 --> 00:02:01,859
If it is me, then I should control it.

15
00:02:01,879 --> 00:02:05,313
So can you say to the body, be bigger, be smaller, etc.

16
00:02:04,953 --> 00:02:05,313
?

17
00:02:05,885 --> 00:02:10,050
And it's the same with feelings, perceptions, your habits.

18
00:02:10,150 --> 00:02:12,413
Are you absolutely in control of your habits?

19
00:02:12,693 --> 00:02:13,653
And so on.

20
00:02:13,674 --> 00:02:14,735
So it's about control.

21
00:02:15,056 --> 00:02:20,241
And then the next question is, is it permanent or impermanent?

22
00:02:20,322 --> 00:02:23,425
So the soul of something everlasting is meant to be permanent.

23
00:02:23,506 --> 00:02:25,427
So it's obviously impermanent.

24
00:02:25,448 --> 00:02:27,610
If it's impermanent, is it satisfactory?

25
00:02:28,010 --> 00:02:29,293
No, it's not satisfactory.

26
